> Big month! Lots happening both in shareable-land and personal doesn't-belong-on-my-blog
> land.

# Stuff I Did
## Ran

I actually broke `thoth` halfway through the month and have not gotten around to fixing it
yet, but strava says 96km?!? Which is much higher than I expected. I have finally been
upping my long run distance (and actually sticking to it), so that accounts for most of
it. But still, good to see, even if I'm still only doing a portion of the plan I laid out
for myself.

## Played

Another pretty slow month on the games front since it's summer in the Netherlands, which
means as much time spent outside as possible. I did have one highlight though:
* [Dead as Disco](https://store.steampowered.com/app/3404260/Dead_as_Disco/), which is everything [Hi-Fi RUSH](rr-hifi-rush) should have been. It is sad
  that it's only in early access, but having already beaten all the bosses in the game so
  far, something about it really scratches an itch for me.

## Read
### Books

I actually only got through *two books* this month, which I take as a bit of a personal
insult. Again this was largely because I spent many nights outside until late, and most of
my reading happens before bed (and was therefore often skipped):
* *[The Secret Commonwealth](https://hardcover.app/books/the-secret-commonwealth)* was... fine. I'll finish the series (already have the third book anyway),
  but it just doesn't have the same spark for me as the original His Dark Materials
  trilogy. I definitely appreciate that the Book of Dust trilogy is decidedly for adults
  and not children, but this one in particular just felt so slow and dull up until the
  last few chapters. Hopefully it ends strong, there's good ideas being built up, but we
  shall see.
* *Harry Potter en de Gevangene van Azkaban* (no link for this one), which is the Dutch
  translation of Prisoner of Azkaban (if that was not obvious). I'm really happy that I'm
  to the point where this can be my before-bed reading rather than something that requires
  intense focus, and my brain slips into full Dutch mode within just a couple sentences.
  Goblet of Fire will be the real test, since the writing gets decidedly more complex.

### Not Books

In a continuing effort to not satiate this section with links to "person rants about how
AI is bullshit," I will limit myself to just the one this month:
* *[AI Mania Is Eviscerating Global Decision-Making](https://ludic.mataroa.blog/blog/ai-mania-is-eviscerating-global-decision-making/)*, by one of my favorite bloggers (Ludic's stuff is linked all over my own blog).
  I continue to flat refuse to use any AI anything at work (and continue to perform my job
  just as competently and efficiently as everyone else), and this piece hit the nail on
  the head for me in regards to just weathering the storm of constant bullshit background
  noise. I've also adopted almost word for word the exact thing that Ludic does:

> When someone asks me for my opinion of AI as a programmer, I recommend saying “Oh, that
> stuff is pretty overblown” and then changing the topic, unless they are in a position
> where their opinion might influence something important.

